<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara was a Fijian high chief from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lau-islands">Lau Islands</a> and leader of the Alliance Party. He became chief minister in 1967 and the country's first prime minister at independence in 1970, holding office until 1992 apart from a short break in 1987. He then served as president from 1993 until he was removed during the crisis of 2000.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kamisese Mara was born on 6 May 1920 at Sawana, Lomaloma, on Vanuabalavu in the Lau archipelago. His father, Ratu Tevita Uluilakeba III, was Tui Nayau and head of the chiefly Vuanirewa clan, and Mara succeeded him as Tui Nayau in 1969. He studied medicine at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otago">Otago</a> from 1942 to 1945, but his great-uncle and mentor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-sir-lala-sukuna">Ratu Sir Lala Sukuna</a>, wishing to groom him for national leadership, arranged for him to study history at Wadham College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he took an MA in 1949. On 11 September 1949 he married Ro Lala, a paramount chief of Rewa in her own right; the marriage lasted more than 53 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Mara served as Chief Minister from 1967 to 1970. With independence the office became Prime Minister, and he held it from 1970 to 1992 apart from one brief interruption in 1987. His Alliance Party lost narrowly to the National Federation Party in March 1977, but the party splintered and the Governor-General called on Mara to form a government, and the Alliance won 36 of 52 seats in a second election that September. In 1987 a multiracial coalition led by Timoci Bavadra defeated him. After two military coups led by Sitiveni Rabuka he was recalled to head an interim administration, and in 1992 he handed power to an elected government.</p><p>Mara was elected Vice-President in June 1992 and became President when Ratu Sir Penaia Ganilau died on 16 December 1993. On 19 May 2000 gunmen led by George Speight seized Parliament and took the Prime Minister, Mahendra Chaudhry, hostage. Mara was taken aboard a naval ship on 28 May and left office, with a resignation retroactive to 29 May 2000. Commodore Frank Bainimarama said the resignation was voluntary, while Mahendra Chaudhry supported Mara's own account that he refused to suspend the Constitution.</p><p>Under him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> crop more than doubled between 1970 and 1987. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-fijians">Indo-Fijians</a> criticised him for not doing more to stop the 1987 coups and for consenting to the 1990 constitution, which guaranteed indigenous Fijian supremacy, and in 1996 he apologised to the Indo-Fijian community for his part in drafting it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his resignation Mara retired to Lakeba and remained a member of the Great Council of Chiefs. He suffered a stroke in 2001 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a> on 18 April 2004. About 200,000 people, almost a quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>'s population, lined the streets during his three-day state funeral, and he was regarded as the father of the nation. He wrote an autobiography, The Pacific Way: A Memoir.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kamisese Mara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Uluilakeba was the father of <b>Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara</b>, the founding father of modern Fiji.</blockquote><a href="/songs/isa-lei">Isa Lei</a></li><li><blockquote>Fiji became independent on 10 October 1970, with <b>Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara</b> as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-fiji">Colony of Fiji</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanua-balavu">Vanua Balavu</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/fiji">Fiji</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-17.22,-178.93&amp;year=1974" aria-label="Vanua Balavu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="0 2803 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M136 3073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M136 3073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-17.22,-178.93&amp;year=1974">What was here in 1974? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
